WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:03.310
Right, Lora, Niki, welcome.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.759
We're starting with the first topic.

3
00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:14.040
Lora, we'll begin with you. We all know the Easypay payment desks,

4
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.550
but today the company is much more than that.

5
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:25.480
Tell us briefly about your mission today and what is special about

6
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:26.669
Varna and the region.

7
00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:29.960
Hello. Can you hear me?

8
00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.989
I don't think so. Can you hear us

9
00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:38.550
now? Now we can. And hello to my beloved city of Varna.

10
00:00:39.399 --> 00:00:43.840
Sunny as always, or maybe I just bring better weather.

11
00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.079
I'm very happy to be here.

12
00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:49.800
I won't go into too much detail.

13
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.480
Everyone pays electricity, water and taxes to Varna Municipality or to other

14
00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.470
municipalities. But Easypay is, in essence, a technology company.

15
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.749
We started operating back in the 1990s.

16
00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:12.989
Our first big company is ePay, from 1999,

17
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:19.960
whose goal back then, and still today,

18
00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.390
is to accept payments over the internet.

19
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.870
That happened five or six years before Easypay even existed.

20
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.520
I can even tell you something interesting that you may not know,

21
00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.720
that the first online purchase in Bulgaria was made precisely through

22
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.629
the ePay system. It was a disc from FSB

23
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:45.200
on 19 November 1999, from an online shop

24
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.349
that no longer exists.

25
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:53.320
But as we launched ePay, little by little we realised

26
00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:57.520
that household bills are what people most want

27
00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.989
to pay quickly, easily, without much hassle.

28
00:02:00.439 --> 00:02:04.759
And five or six years later, in 2004-2005,

29
00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:08.720
in a very hot summer, an elderly woman

30
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.480
- I've told this story many times, but since we've started,

31
00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.830
I'll tell it again. Maybe I haven't told it in Varna.

32
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:21.550
An elderly woman faints at a desk of Electricity Distribution Sofia,

33
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.470
today part of Eurohold.

34
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:33.000
So, in order to have more places where household bills can be

35
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.760
paid, the distribution company said: "Look, we already pay you

36
00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.800
some things through ePay, so let's set up more

37
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.760
points." We first did it with two Bulgarian banks,

38
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.879
which, however, started to get annoyed at some point,

39
00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:49.800
because people went to ask why their water was so expensive,

40
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:55.480
their electricity too, while the others waited behind, and at some point

41
00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:00.760
we started opening our own desks, and today we have 4,000 minus

42
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:07.280
twenty or so, say 3,973. In Varna we have quite a few

43
00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.709
sites, around 240

44
00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:17.120
Easypay desks. And they work very well.

45
00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.680
Besides bills, what we've been trying to do

46
00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:24.599
in recent years is position Easypay as a place

47
00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:28.280
where you can pay for all kinds of things and get

48
00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.069
all kinds of financial services, including

49
00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:38.680
well, of course, money transfers, taxes, vignettes, and we now open

50
00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:45.120
accounts for individuals and companies, and for 5-6 years we've had

51
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.750
prepaid cards, that is, debit cards.

52
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.760
Today we issue them with Mastercard, with BC,

53
00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.879
so anyone who wants a debit card can get one with us

54
00:03:55.879 --> 00:03:57.429
in 5 minutes.

55
00:03:58.599 --> 00:04:04.030
So, in general, that is Easypay today.

56
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:10.159
Great. And since the room is mostly local

57
00:04:10.159 --> 00:04:15.799
companies, what specific opportunities and conveniences do you give

58
00:04:15.799 --> 00:04:22.400
local businesses? That's a great question, because we are currently

59
00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:27.830
working on a fairly large project aimed specifically at business.

60
00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:31.919
As I mentioned, businesses can have accounts with us,

61
00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:35.199
make mass payments, and have cards linked to those

62
00:04:35.199 --> 00:04:40.150
accounts. This often makes things easier for companies with many employees,

63
00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:44.400
for example logistics companies that need to give cards to their

64
00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:49.320
drivers or staff so they can cover various expenses with company

65
00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:53.000
cards. They are tracked very easily, money is transferred very

66
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:59.080
easily. What we're doing now is making it easier for companies,

67
00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:03.560
especially in less populated places, to come and

68
00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:09.150
open accounts at our desks, and they can also get POS terminals

69
00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.039
to accept payments. We already have, by the way,

70
00:05:12.039 --> 00:05:13.720
200 ATMs across the country.

71
00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:18.240
This morning I visited one of our offices here in

72
00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.440
Varna, and it turns out we have more and more companies

73
00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.639
that collect cash, and when they want to deposit

74
00:05:25.639 --> 00:05:29.160
that cash, they can do it at our ATM.

75
00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:34.319
You insert the card. Our ATM is a modern one, with big

76
00:05:34.319 --> 00:05:36.469
trays that hold a lot of cash.

77
00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:41.800
So we offer these services to companies too,

78
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.910
if they need them. Great.

79
00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:52.319
Niki, you're from Varna, tell us a bit about what Axiomi does and

80
00:05:52.319 --> 00:05:57.400
what the pros and challenges are of running a global business

81
00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.950
from Varna. Hello from me, too.

82
00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.240
Axiomi is actually a product building studio, whatever that means.

83
00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:10.800
We actually help businesses build their digital products and

84
00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.589
transform their processes,

85
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.029
so that they are as efficient as possible.

86
00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.350
Our focus is mainly on three industries: fintech, fuel tech and logistics,

87
00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:26.639
and I can say we work with the larger companies on the market in

88
00:06:26.639 --> 00:06:31.160
Bulgaria. This year we also managed to enter the German market

89
00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:36.880
and the Middle East market, however hard this

90
00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:42.350
year was. In general, we solve two problems.

91
00:06:42.759 --> 00:06:47.800
Expertise where it's needed, to build complex business processes

92
00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:49.749
and to support

93
00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:52.639
complex software projects.

94
00:06:52.639 --> 00:06:57.680
And the second direction: design, prototyping and validation of

95
00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:03.960
ideas and concepts directly with the end users of the

96
00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:08.270
service. What are the challenges of doing this from Varna?

97
00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:15.800
Huge ones, mostly logistical, but overall it's a daily struggle.

98
00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:19.110
It takes a lot of travelling, a lot of contacts, and

99
00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:25.599
the problem when you're a company from Varna is that most companies

100
00:07:25.599 --> 00:07:28.759
abroad either haven't heard of Varna, or have heard of it,

101
00:07:28.759 --> 00:07:33.639
but not enough, and they know Sofia in Bulgaria, and it's very hard to

102
00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:38.560
imagine working with a company in such a remote

103
00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:44.520
place; it takes more effort and investment in building

104
00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:51.240
trust, which membership in this type of associations and

105
00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:52.790
chambers helps with quite a lot.

106
00:07:55.720 --> 00:08:01.199
Thank you. What does Axiomi contribute to local business, and how

107
00:08:01.199 --> 00:08:07.230
do you help regional companies grow too?

108
00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:12.520
Honestly, we don't have many clients at local level.

109
00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:16.080
Mainly because we have focused on fintech

110
00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:22.350
companies and fuel tech, which are headquartered mostly in Sofia.

111
00:08:23.159 --> 00:08:28.000
Here locally we've worked with companies like ICard,

112
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:32.000
myPOS and others, as well as some local startups,

113
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.230
which we helped to build their product

114
00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.120
offering and positioning in a software context,

115
00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.640
of course. But at the moment, the dynamics in the city are not very strong in

116
00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:47.750
this direction, for objective reasons.

117
00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:55.160
Lora, Easypay has a strong presence among Bulgarians in the

118
00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:58.629
United Kingdom. What were the biggest challenges

119
00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.190
in entering that market, and which services are most in demand there?

120
00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:08.190
We have many clients from the United Kingdom,

121
00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:11.030
because they buy properties in Bansko,

122
00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.480
but yes, we work with quite a lot of British people.

123
00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:19.839
The truth is that we couldn't get a foothold in the UK the way

124
00:09:19.839 --> 00:09:23.279
we wanted. We had plans three or four years ago,

125
00:09:23.279 --> 00:09:24.790
but they fell through a bit.

126
00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:27.590
Still, we work mainly

127
00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:33.720
with British people who live or have businesses here,

128
00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:40.320
or own property here. But besides that, at Easypay we do

129
00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.230
a lot of money transfers. That is one of our main businesses.

130
00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:51.200
Just a few days ago we announced a very important partnership for

131
00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:55.590
us, which we worked on for a very long time - with Western Union.

132
00:09:56.079 --> 00:09:59.680
So today Easypay works with the biggest money transfer

133
00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:02.389
operators in the whole world.

134
00:10:03.079 --> 00:10:09.800
In August alone, for the Varna region alone, 76,000

135
00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:13.030
money transfers were sent and received.

136
00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:17.069
So from that point of view Easypay is a good partner,

137
00:10:17.959 --> 00:10:23.240
and we have a very large network, not only in the UK

138
00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:25.949
but worldwide, in 200 territories and countries.

139
00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:30.200
That's a very good transition to my next question,

140
00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:34.800
which is: which other international markets are you looking at, and how

141
00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:38.069
does digitalisation help you cross borders?

142
00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:43.670
We see ourselves as fanatically digital,

143
00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:49.519
even though a large part of our business actually comes from cash

144
00:10:49.519 --> 00:10:53.629
payments, but that is simply how the market has been over the years.

145
00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:56.310
And in our understanding,

146
00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:02.440
we must offer the customer every possible

147
00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:06.000
option to pay and make a transaction,

148
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.560
to pay whatever they want to pay and choose the one

149
00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:14.120
that suits them best. So they can do it at a desk,

150
00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:15.800
they can do it through ePay.

151
00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:19.550
Five years ago we launched payments through Viber,

152
00:11:20.079 --> 00:11:23.160
so bills can also be paid through Viber.

153
00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:26.959
Last year, in December, we processed 1 million paid bills

154
00:11:26.959 --> 00:11:29.399
through Viber, which of course, against everything

155
00:11:29.399 --> 00:11:32.670
that gets paid, is a small percentage, but a nice one.

156
00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:36.590
Payments through banking

157
00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:40.760
apps also largely go through us.

158
00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:45.920
So overall we see ourselves as a digital company, and in terms of

159
00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:49.560
expansion we have quite a few offices in Serbia.

160
00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:52.200
We have them in Cyprus, in Spain, in Greece,

161
00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:56.320
of course, wherever there is a Bulgarian diaspora,

162
00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:03.120
we have offices. We are considering developing two or three markets around

163
00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:05.350
Bulgaria, but I won't name them yet.

164
00:12:06.279 --> 00:12:08.110
So we'll wait for you to share.

165
00:12:08.959 --> 00:12:12.320
Niki, you mentioned your international experience.

166
00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:15.550
Could you tell us a bit more, especially about

167
00:12:17.199 --> 00:12:21.110
how you entered the Middle East, especially in such turbulent

168
00:12:21.959 --> 00:12:27.360
times? We entered the Middle East specifically before the turbulent

169
00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:31.509
times, which made the market difficult at the moment,

170
00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:37.360
but what I managed to build is stable partnerships with

171
00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:41.720
companies, which helped me enter the market this way

172
00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:45.639
as a start-up, founded in 2024,

173
00:12:45.639 --> 00:12:48.199
not long ago, built, of course, on the foundations

174
00:12:48.199 --> 00:12:53.350
of my consulting services as an individual, but a young company.

175
00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:58.720
I had to prove to my future clients and partners

176
00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:00.949
that they can trust me, as I said earlier,

177
00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:04.959
which often requires someone else to vouch for you,

178
00:13:04.959 --> 00:13:09.760
so to speak. So on the basis of joint ventures

179
00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:16.030
that I founded, we managed to enter Kuwait and worked with

180
00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:20.710
Saudi Telecom Company, where we advised them on how to build

181
00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:24.040
their software development processes, literally,

182
00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:29.240
which for me was super strange - a company of that scale needing

183
00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:31.949
exactly that kind of service from a guy from Varna,

184
00:13:32.399 --> 00:13:38.199
but it's a fact that we contributed to it. And the story

185
00:13:38.199 --> 00:13:43.199
of entering the German market is roughly similar, where we currently work with

186
00:13:43.199 --> 00:13:48.440
the largest parking operator, and they needed to

187
00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:54.360
rewrite their entire core software, with which they managed

188
00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:57.069
the car parks, which is a very complex and heavy migration,

189
00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:01.470
which, if not planned properly, can endanger the entire business.

190
00:14:02.079 --> 00:14:06.800
And so, I'd say that for about a year now we've been doing very well,

191
00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:08.790
but the foundation

192
00:14:09.759 --> 00:14:12.079
on which I am currently betting and investing

193
00:14:12.079 --> 00:14:16.240
is building relationships, building trust where

194
00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:19.399
it is needed, joint ventures and partnerships,

195
00:14:19.399 --> 00:14:22.550
so that they can prove themselves.

196
00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:29.720
How does your approach differ across regions, and of course,

197
00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:33.350
do you have ambitions to enter the British market too?

198
00:14:35.959 --> 00:14:41.959
Of course, since I'm a member of the Bulgarian-British Chamber of Commerce,

199
00:14:41.959 --> 00:14:46.360
and by the way I'm something of an exception, because two years ago, shortly

200
00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:48.629
after I had de facto founded the company,

201
00:14:49.759 --> 00:14:55.590
I applied for membership with you. And it was an exception for this kind of

202
00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:58.430
nano company to be admitted.

203
00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:01.759
Accordingly, I again had to show a reference,

204
00:15:01.759 --> 00:15:06.759
which was great, because people's impression of the chamber is

205
00:15:06.759 --> 00:15:12.069
that you don't get in so easily, which gives you some credit of trust.

206
00:15:12.839 --> 00:15:16.320
So the British market is very interesting for us.

207
00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:20.519
For organic reasons we ended up elsewhere this year, and it was not

208
00:15:20.519 --> 00:15:25.040
a focus. I personally fine-tune where I direct my efforts.

209
00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:31.639
At the moment, from the British market we mostly

210
00:15:31.639 --> 00:15:34.110
draw know-how and draw

211
00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:41.199
an understanding of how it's done, since it is the Mecca of

212
00:15:41.199 --> 00:15:45.000
fintech companies. We have project managers and companies

213
00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:50.120
we work with there, with whom we source this kind of know-how for

214
00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:53.670
Bulgarian companies, but we don't sell directly.

215
00:15:55.880 --> 00:15:58.600
The next question is for both of you.

216
00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:02.680
At which moments has the partnership with the British-Bulgarian

217
00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:05.430
Chamber of Commerce brought you the greatest value?

218
00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:10.710
Was it networking, contacts or trust?

219
00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:15.309
At the risk of repeating myself.

220
00:16:16.279 --> 00:16:21.360
In my case, since it is very specific to the size of the company

221
00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:24.920
and at the stage we are at, for me the greatest added

222
00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:29.990
value was the trust that the people I met with

223
00:16:30.759 --> 00:16:32.829
showed when they found out

224
00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:37.399
that I am a member of the British-Bulgarian Chamber of Commerce.

225
00:16:37.399 --> 00:16:41.279
That definitely works. I wasn't able to take advantage of most of the

226
00:16:41.279 --> 00:16:44.910
events this year that were organized by the chamber.

227
00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:49.120
But at the few I attended, I managed to make the necessary

228
00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:55.120
contacts with people we later did projects with.

229
00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:59.790
So the internal Bulgarian network also works for

230
00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:06.360
development. Opportunities are everywhere; you must seek them and

231
00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:08.229
know exactly what you want.

232
00:17:08.679 --> 00:17:12.720
So I see added value in these areas:

233
00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:17.000
building trust, building the internal network, and

234
00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:20.160
then, perhaps, the next stage, which is for our

235
00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:24.190
company, for it to help us enter this market.

236
00:17:27.319 --> 00:17:31.510
We took advantage of almost all the events,

237
00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:35.640
we are a very active member, and what he

238
00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:38.830
told you, those are our pluses too.

239
00:17:39.559 --> 00:17:41.640
We were at many events.

240
00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:44.030
We were in London too. It was a very nice event.

241
00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:47.510
The truth is that for us the value

242
00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:53.080
lies largely in the Bulgarian companies we get to know and

243
00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:57.790
have good relations with, creating business opportunities.

244
00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:03.120
So yes, that is perhaps the most useful part.

245
00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:06.150
They asked us for references too, at least two,

246
00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:13.280
but it happened quickly. If you had to give one short

247
00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:18.440
but valuable piece of advice to a Varna company that is now planning

248
00:18:18.440 --> 00:18:22.870
to enter a foreign market, what would it be?

249
00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:30.280
We definitely live in difficult times, so a certain dose of

250
00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:35.760
stoicism is needed. For me the main advice would be: don't lose focus,

251
00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:41.240
be persistent and invest in building your network and

252
00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:42.600
trust every single day.

253
00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:45.109
Sooner or later it will start to pay off.

254
00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:49.360
Entering a given market is not the ultimate goal.

255
00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:51.360
I see it more as a result.

256
00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:55.510
Rather, the road to that stage is the valuable experience.

257
00:18:56.039 --> 00:18:59.990
But be patient, because we live in harder times.

258
00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:08.280
And I would add that new markets are sometimes easier with

259
00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:10.950
partnerships. At least that is how we have always operated.

260
00:19:11.799 --> 00:19:17.799
Because good partners in markets who know the markets and

261
00:19:17.799 --> 00:19:19.510
who are motivated

262
00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:23.840
for two companies to move forward together

263
00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:29.480
always reduce the risk. So we operate abroad a lot

264
00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:32.830
through partnerships, and so far it has worked.

265
00:19:34.919 --> 00:19:37.640
Now we give the floor to the audience.

266
00:19:37.640 --> 00:19:41.670
If any of you have questions for our panelists,

267
00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:43.910
now is the moment to ask them.

268
00:19:53.480 --> 00:20:00.240
Yes, thank you. Since we are a British

269
00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:04.880
company that is only now going to work in Bulgaria,

270
00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:06.910
we have some problems with

271
00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:13.640
payments, let's say, because Britain is

272
00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:15.630
outside the European Union and so on.

273
00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:21.070
I assume you had similar problems

274
00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:23.589
when you went to England.

275
00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:28.240
Did you solve this problem through a partner, meaning

276
00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:29.840
did you partner with a local company?

277
00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:34.990
How did it work? We are talking about orders of the

278
00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:41.790
local authority type or public procurement.

279
00:20:44.039 --> 00:20:45.640
I don't think we had any problems.

280
00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:49.640
The point is to have accounts in the right banks or the right

281
00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:51.830
institutions. It shouldn't be an issue,

282
00:20:52.799 --> 00:20:57.000
but if you like, you can tell me in detail later and we'll see how we can

283
00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:00.750
be useful to you, or I can give you some advice.

284
00:21:01.240 --> 00:21:05.120
In general the world is quite open now, it's getting quite easy,

285
00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:08.559
right. It's a fact that fees are higher when you're not in the Union.

286
00:21:08.559 --> 00:21:12.200
Yes, we had a solution. I just wanted to see if it's the right

287
00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:14.679
solution, but it may be so.

288
00:21:14.679 --> 00:21:19.640
For example, we are now preparing to enter a neighbouring market

289
00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:24.750
that is in the Union but not in the euro, and it has a procedure

290
00:21:25.159 --> 00:21:28.919
so that we can register accounts and operate in

291
00:21:28.919 --> 00:21:34.559
another currency. It's not that it doesn't exist, but it's quite normal.

292
00:21:34.559 --> 00:21:37.960
People often ask us: "Why do we now have to submit so many

293
00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:39.520
documents?

294
00:21:39.520 --> 00:21:41.039
Why do you want this from me here?

295
00:21:41.039 --> 00:21:46.840
Why that?" Because we and all Bulgarian banks and most

296
00:21:46.840 --> 00:21:50.279
companies that handle money are all under the law,

297
00:21:50.279 --> 00:21:54.200
under BNB supervision, and we watch everything we do,

298
00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:55.910
so as to avoid any

299
00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:58.640
kind of abuse.

300
00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:02.279
There are many people in this world, unfortunately, who want to commit

301
00:22:02.279 --> 00:22:07.229
such abuses. We have a whole department that deals only with this.

302
00:22:08.320 --> 00:22:10.559
So yes, there is bureaucracy.

303
00:22:10.559 --> 00:22:13.109
If you like, you can tell me in more detail later.

304
00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:19.510
Thank you.

